// **`Any`** (ADR-0076): a value whose type travels with it, so a procedure can take an argument whose
// type it does not know and read it back safely. W4's eighth sub-wave, and the second half of RTTI.
//
// `any_of(*x)` erases: it builds `Any{type, data}` where `type` is a `*Type_Info` describing `x` and
// `data` points at `x`. `any_as(a, T)` recovers: it checks the `Any`'s type is `T` and returns the value,
// or **traps** if it is not. The check is the whole point — a read that ignored the type would make `Any`
// a `void*` with extra steps.
//
// Three decisions are visible here, each argued in ADR-0076 and ADR-0077.
//
// **The erasing conversion is allowed only here.** `any_of(*p)` turns a `*Point` into the `*u8` that
// `Any.data` holds, and a bare `cast(*u8, p)` stays refused (E0232). A general pointer cast would make
// every pointer type interconvertible, so a wrong pointee type would be a silent wrong read — the
// reinterpretation ADR-0045 §1 confined to `union`, put on every pointer unmarked. Nothing is
// reinterpreted here: a pointer's bits do not depend on its pointee, so the erasure emits no code.
//
// **The read back is checked by a stable identity, not by a pointer.** Two `type_info(T)` calls have
// different addresses (each spills its own slot, ADR-0075 §2), so `any_as` cannot compare `*Type_Info`
// pointers. ADR-0077 added `Type_Info.id` — the type's pool id, the identity the whole compiler already
// uses and identical in both engines because they share one pool — and `any_as` compares that. Comparing
// by `name` would be *unsound*: a local `Point` and an imported one share a name and are different types
// (ADR-0015 §1).
//
// **A mismatch traps, matching `variant`.** `any_as(a, Wrong)` traps exactly as reading the wrong case of
// a tagged `variant` does (ADR-0068 §4) — a tag says which read is valid, and the wrong one traps rather
// than reinterpreting. That path is exercised in `differential.rs` (a trap aborts the process, which a
// corpus file that must exit 0 cannot show); here every `any_as` matches.
//
// Nine assertions, summing to 767 so any one failing changes the exit value. Both engines must agree —
// and the native one has to materialise a `Type_Info` in static-ish form, erase a pointer and read it
// back, all of which could fail independently of the VM.
#import "Basic";

/// Two fields, so a transposed read would put a value in the wrong place rather than merely working.
Point :: struct {
    x: s64;
    y: s64;
}

/// A different struct with the *same shape* (two `s64`s, size 16, align 8), which is what makes the
/// identity check earn its keep: `size`/`alignment`/`kind` cannot tell it from `Point`, only `id` can.
Pair :: struct {
    a: s64;
    b: s64;
}

/// Reads an `Any` it is told holds a `Point`, and sums it — the type-erased-argument use case.
sum_point :: (a: Any) -> s64 {
    p := any_as(a, Point);
    return p.x + p.y;
}

main :: () {
    n := 0;

    // Round-trip a struct through `Any` and back.
    p: Point;
    p.x = 3;
    p.y = 4;
    a := any_of(*p);
    q := any_as(a, Point);
    if q.x == 3 {
        n = n + 1;
    }
    if q.y == 4 {
        n = n + 2;
    }

    // Through a procedure that took the `Any` without knowing its type — passing an already-built `Any`.
    if sum_point(a) == 7 {
        n = n + 4;
    }

    // The **implicit coercion**, and it takes the value now (ADR-0189 §1). `sum_point` wants an `Any`, so
    // `p` — a `Point`, not a pointer — is described and materialised at the call. `takes(any_of(*x))` and
    // `takes(x)` are the same call, which is the ergonomic half ADR-0076 §1 promised, finally in the form
    // it promised it.
    //
    // **This line used to read `sum_point(*p)`**, because ADR-0076 §4 could not materialise a value's
    // address, so a caller passed a pointer and the coercion erased the pointee. That is withdrawn: the
    // implicit coercion describes the argument's own type, so `sum_point(*p)` would now pass an `Any`
    // describing a `*Point` and `any_as(a, Point)` inside would trap. Passing the value is what the file
    // wanted to write all along.
    if sum_point(p) == 7 {
        n = n + 512;
    }

    // The `type` field is a real `*Type_Info`, readable through the `Any`.
    if a.type.size == 16 {
        n = n + 8;
    }
    if a.type.kind == Type_Info_Kind.STRUCT {
        n = n + 16;
    }

    // A **builtin** erased and recovered — the path that has no declaration.
    m: s64;
    m = 99;
    b := any_of(*m);
    r := any_as(b, s64);
    if r == 99 {
        n = n + 32;
    }
    if b.type.id == type_info(s64).id {
        n = n + 64;
    }

    // `Point` and `Pair` are the same shape but **different types**: their `id`s differ, which is exactly
    // what a sound `any_as` relies on and a name/size comparison would get wrong.
    ip := type_info(Point);
    ir := type_info(Pair);
    if ip.id != ir.id {
        n = n + 128;
    }

    // Every assertion: 767 (255 + the 512 coercion bit).
    if n == 767 {
        exit(0);
    }
    exit(1);
}
